<http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n111580> a ns2:CourtesyFaculty,
        ns2:UFCurrentEntity,
        ns2:UFEntity,
        ns8:Thing,
        <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Agent>,
        <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Person> ;
    ns5:modTime "2010-10-26T12:33:47"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ns1:educationalTraining <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n106071>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n82549> ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "dafocks@epi.ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 294-5148" ;
    ns1:researchOverview """<p>Early work at ARS involved bio-control and the development of computer simulation models designed to help in the control <i>Aedes aegypti,</i> the vector of yellow fever and dengue haemorrhagic fever, and vectors of Venezuelan Equine Encephalitis arising from the rice agro-ecosystem.</p>\r
<p>Later work at ARS focused on the development of validated and weather-driven epidemiologic models of dengue transmission.&nbsp; These models have been widely distributed for basic research, control, risk assessment, and vaccine trials.&nbsp; They are also used for control and research purposes by WHO and PAHO, and operationally by some 20 countries in the Americas, the Pacific, and S.E. Asia.&nbsp;</p>\r
<p>Recent research efforts have largely been directed toward (1) developing validated assessments of the potential consequences of climate change and control strategies for dengue, malaria, schistosomiasis, and Lyme disease and, (2) in collaboration with ministries of health in S.E. Asia, the National Oceanographic and Atmospheric Administration&rsquo;s Office of Global Programs (NOAA-OGP) and WHO, developing and implementing dengue early warning and control systems based on El Ni&ntilde;o/Southern Oscillation (ENSO) forecasts.&nbsp; For the past several years the focus has been on (3) a pilot dengue control program using a targeted intervention in three types of especially productive containers; the venue is the city of Yogyakarta in south central Java with a population of one-half million.&nbsp; Also under way through the end of 2010 is a Gates Foundation-funded study with UC-Davis and Colorado State involving development of a web-based Dengue Decision Support System and enhancements and recoding of the dengue models.&nbsp;</p>""" .

