<http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n387583> a ns2:UFCurrentEntity,
        ns2:UFEntity,
        ns1:GraduateStudent ;
    ns5:modTime "2013-04-08T15:30:00"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ns1:awardOrHonor <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n245891>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n4316> ;
    ns1:educationalTraining <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n471116>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n51005> ;
    ns1:geographicFocus <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Florida>,
        <http://dbpedia.org/resource/Ohio> ;
    ns1:hasAttendeeRole <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n189313>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n271312>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n38652>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n86675>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n872> ;
    ns1:hasTeacherRole <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n117966> ;
    ns1:overview "With a background focusing on biology,&nbsp;engineering,&nbsp;ecology, chemistry, and&nbsp;hydrology&nbsp;I enjoy working on projects related to environmental pollution prevention and remediation, contaminant fate and transport, and sustainable watershed management. <br /><br />I have worked on a number of research and remediation projects at: &nbsp;Ohio EPA, Univ. of Cincinnati's environmental engineering research labs, USEPA federal research facility, and currently at the Univ. of Florida's engineering research facilities. &nbsp;The work has covered various subject matter related to water quality and proper resource management, and specifically included: surface water and watershed quality field sampling and modeling, oil and heavy metal&nbsp;contaminated sediment remediation, drinking water disinfection-by-product (DBP) formation, metal speciation changes in aging pipe infrastructure, field and soil research on the effects of nutrient pollution in small scale lotic systems, fate and transport of endocrine disrupting compounds (EDCs) in waste water, and air quality effects at biosolid application sites.<br /><br />Currently, I am pursuing a doctorate degree in environmental engineering sciences at the University of Florida.&nbsp; Project subject matter focuses on human and environmental implications of alternative waste water reuse practices in the the state, and how long term land application of effluent can influence phosphorus fate, transport, and sustainability as a valuable resource.<br /><br />Areas of specialization: Water quality, nutrient polluton, phosphorus sustainable and maangement, biodegradation, watershed management, field sampling, analytical chemistry" ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "gweinkam1@ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(513) 404-4074" ;
    ns1:researchOverview "Investigating the fate, transport, and potential long term (&gt;20 years) fate of phosphorus in treated waste water effluent around the state of Florida." .

