<http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n765312> a ns2:UFEntity,
        ns1:Center,
        ns8:Thing,
        <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Agent>,
        <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Organization> ;
    ns5:modTime "2011-08-03T15:31:24"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ns1:overview """<p>The Center for Environmental and Human Toxicology serves as the focal point at the University of Florida for activities concerning the effects of chemicals on the environment, human and animal health.</p>\r
<p>The Center serves as an interface between basic research and its application for evaluation of human health and environmental risks. This interface includes an educational component to transfer this knowledge to producers, consumers, and regulators. The research and teaching activities of the Center provide a resource for the State of Florida to identify and reduce risks associated with environmental pollution, food contamination, and workplace hazards. Development and improvement of risk assessment methods as well as toxicity testing and elucidation of mechanisms of action of chemical-induced adverse health effects are all activities of the Center that serve as resources for the State of Florida and the nation. The Center provides a forum for the discussion of specific and general problems concerning the potential adverse human health effects associated with chemical exposure. Using the interpretive skills of scientists and clinicians from various health disciplines, better decisions can be made for the protection of public health.</p>""" ;
    ns1:subOrganizationWithin <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n237306> .

<http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n765320> a ns2:UFEntity,
        ns1:AcademicDepartment,
        ns1:Department,
        ns8:Thing,
        <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Agent>,
        <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Organization> ;
    ns5:modTime "2011-08-04T08:49:26"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ns1:organizationForTraining <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n23226>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n53044> ;
    ns1:overview "The primary mission of the department is a commitment to scholarly research, teaching and service to advance the state of knowledge in information systems and supply chain management and to train future leaders for professional and academic careers." ;
    ns1:subOrganizationWithin <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n987233> .

<http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n765323> a ns2:UFEntity,
        ns1:Center,
        ns8:Thing,
        <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Agent>,
        <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Organization> ;
    ns5:modTime "2013-10-09T17:43:52"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ns1:affiliatedOrganization <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n207803>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n88293> ;
    ns1:overview """<p>The SWAMP (Software and Analysis of Advanced Materials Processing Center) in the College of Engineering at the University of Florida features interdisciplinary activities aimed at understanding, optimizing, and developing new techniques for the manufacture and design of integrated circuit devices. The center is devoted to understanding and modeling fundamental properties of the materials and devices involved in micro- and nano-electronics. Both experimental and theoretical research is vital to this vision. The SWAMP center performs systematic, fundamental experiments designed to verify models of the physics and chemistry of the materials, structures, and processes. The models developed are licensed to industry to provide technology transfer of the center's research results. Through experimentation, the center develops a better understanding of the changes, on the atomic scale, that occur in the materials used in microelectronics when they are processed. The center uses this understanding to develop physically based models that can be inserted into process and device simulators that allow one to perform accurate virtual investigations of advanced micro- and nano-electronics.</p>\r
<p>This center features interdisciplinary activities combining analytic techniques from materials science to modeling and device design. This interdisciplinary approach has proved valuable to industry since it has provided software that models the physics and chemistry of the manufacturing process, and experimental work that aims at calibrating the software models. One of the acknowledged strengths of the program is that the students learn techniques and capabilities interdisciplinary teams.</p>\r
<p>The research funding is close to 1 million dollars annually. This funding is approximately 90% industrially based, including support from <a href="http://www.sematech.org/">SEMATECH</a> and the <a href="http://www.src.org/">Semiconductor Research Corporation</a> (SRC). Since 1992, additional support has been provided directly from companies such as <a href="http://www.amd.com/us-en/">Advanced Micro Devices</a>, <a href="http://www.axcelis.com/">Axcelis</a>, <a href="http://www.harris.com/">Harris</a>, IBIS, <a href="http://www.ibm.com/us/">IBM</a>, <a href="http://www.intel.com/">Intel</a>, <a href="http://www.ise.com/">ISE</a>, <a href="http://www.lucent.com/">Lucent</a>, <a href="http://www.motorola.com/">Motorola</a>, <a href="http://www.silvaco.com/">Silvaco</a>, <a href="http://www.ti.com/">Texas Instruments</a> and <a href="http://www.varianinc.com/cgi-bin/nav?/">Varian</a>. The SWAMP Center has also worked closely with government agencies including <a href="http://www.nsf.gov/">NSF</a>, <a href="http://www.nist.gov/">NIST</a>, <a href="http://www.nasa.gov/home/">NASA</a>, <a href="http://www.ornl.gov/">Oak Ridge National Laboratories</a>, <a href="http://www.sandia.gov/">Sandia National Laboratories</a> and other universities including <a href="http://www.stanford.edu/">Stanford</a>, <a href="http://www.caltech.edu/">California Institute of Technology</a>, <a href="http://www.utexas.edu/">University of Texas at Austin</a> and <a href="http://www.anu.edu.au/">Australian National University</a>.</p>\r
<p>One of the Center's strengths has been technology transfer to industry. Software has been developed (Florida Object Oriented Process Simulator, FLOOPS), which has been distributed around the world and has been commercialized by ISE. The software project has received the SRC Technical Excellence Award.</p>""" ;
    ns1:subOrganizationWithin <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n1278118> .

