<http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n108084> a ns2:UFCurrentEntity,
        ns2:UFEntity,
        ns1:FacultyMember,
        ns8:Thing,
        <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Agent>,
        <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Person> ;
    ns1:hasLeaderRole <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n26141> ;
    ns1:hasRole <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n1632915700>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n320791251>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n89733270> ;
    ns1:hasTeacherRole <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n1126371675>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n975543571> ;
    ns1:overview """<p>The innate immune system is the first line of defense against microbial pathogens including bacteria, fungi and viruses. A collection of specialized cells including the <strong>professional phagocytes</strong> (<strong>macrophages</strong>, <strong>dendritic cells [DCs]</strong> and neutrophils) scan the body for the presence of microorganisms. Among the phagocytes, macrophages and DCs are also <strong>antigen presenting cells [APCs]</strong> which direct the specific (adaptive) immune response by presenting microbe-derived antigens to lymphocytes. Deficiencies in APC function can lead to overall immune deficiency and greater risk of infections. In contrast, excessive activation of APCs can lead to dangerous <strong>inflammation</strong> and in some cases autoimmune disease. The Wallet Lab studies regulation of APC function in both human immunodeficiency virus type 1 [HIV-1] infection (macrophages) and autoimmune diabetes (DCs).</p>\r
<p>In <strong>HIV-1 infection</strong>, macrophages serve many purposes and contribute to disease pathogenesis. As a direct target of HIV-1 infection, macrophages can contribute to the <strong>viral reservoir</strong>, a place where HIV-1 'hides out' during therapy. Viral reservoirs are the single greatest barrier to a cure for HIV-1 infection and a major research objective of our group is to determine new strategies to<strong> selectively eliminate reservoir cells</strong>. In addition to their role in persistent infection, macrophages also become excessively activated during HIV-1 infection. This activation contributes to chronic, systemic<strong> inflammation</strong> which causes increased morbidity/mortality due to end organ diseases - most prominently cardiovascular disease. We study the mechanisms by which <strong>HIV-1 augments macrophage activation</strong> with the goal of reducing chronic inflammation in HIV-1 patients</p>\r
<p><strong>Type 1 diabetes</strong> ([T1D], juvenile diabetes) is an autoimmune disease typified by uncontrolled blood glucose due to loss of insulin production by 'beta cells' in the pancreas. Beta cell destruction/dysfunction occurs when adaptive immunity becomes focused on self antigens rather than foreign antigens. <strong>DCs</strong> play the most central role in honing the adaptive immune response by priming T lymphocytes to respond to specific antigens. As many as 40&nbsp;human genes confer risk for T1D and other autoimmune diseases (<strong>risk alleles</strong>). One of the most prominent risk alleles is a mutated variant of the gene <em><strong>PTPN22</strong></em> which encodes the protein <strong>Lyp</strong>. Normally Lyp is a negative regulator of immune cell activation, however the risk allele of Lyp has a mutation that impairs its normal function. We are studying the specific mechanism(s) by which Lyp regulates DC function and how the mutant allele may contribute to T1D through altered DC function.</p>""" ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "mawallet@pathology.ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 273-8164" .

