<http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n1967914036> a ns2:UFCurrentEntity,
        ns2:UFEntity,
        ns1:FacultyMember,
        ns8:Thing,
        <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Agent>,
        <http://xmlns.com/foaf/0.1/Person> ;
    ns5:modTime "2011-05-05T15:46:25"^^xsd:dateTime ;
    ns1:awardOrHonor <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n169346>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n42171> ;
    ns1:educationalTraining <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n30312>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n352098>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n37529> ;
    ns1:hasRole <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n1605604897>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n2062504211>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n825059971> ;
    ns1:overview "My research is largely focused on study to understand the mechanism of Alzheimer&rsquo;s disease (AD) and related neurodegenerative disorders. From 1992, I have continuously worked in this research field with the aspect to biochemistry. AD is a most common form of dementia, a general term for the loss of memory and other intellectual abilities serious enough to interfere with daily life. Several genes have been implicated in AD, most notably those encoding amyloid precursor protein (APP), presenilin 1 (PS1), presenilin 2 (PS2), and apolipoprotein E (APOE). Through my contribution, in part, several antibodies that specifically recognize these encoding proteins were produced.<br />" ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "naruhiko7@gmail.com" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 273-9661" ;
    ns1:researchOverview """<p>&nbsp;In 1996, my colleagues and I first demonstrated that PS1 and its fragments have an abnormal gel-electrophoretic property. In addition to the amyloid plaques, neurofibrillary tangles composed of phosphorylated tau protein are a key neuropathological hallmark of AD. After discovery of mutations in the MAPT gene encoding the tau proein in Frontotemporal Dementia and Parkinsonism linked to chromosome 17 (FTDP-17) in 1998, I turned my focus on studying tau protein.</p>\r
<p>As physiological function, tau protein has a well-established role in promoting the stabilization of microtubules. However, the precise role of tau aggregation in the different stages of neurodegenerative disease pathology is not yet fully understood. My research effort is focused on discovering the mechanisms of disease pathology, and development of better diagnostics and therapeutics using the in vitro and in vivo model systems.&nbsp; In addition, because tau pathology is closely related to the progression of dementia, tau protein may have an important role for cognitive function. Research aimed at understanding neural mechanisms underlying memory impairment and dementia is part of my future interest.</p>""" .

