        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n27452> ;
    ns1:hasTeacherRole <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n1247979310>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n1521983206>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n1810834162>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n218258527>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n224723874>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n827178099>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n962077089> ;
    ns1:overview "Sean Hanretta received his BA from the Colorado College in 1994 and his Ph.D. in History from the University of Wisconsin-Madison in 2003. His specialty is the intellectual and cultural history of West Africa, particularly the history of Islam and of religion in West Africa more generally. He also works on the history of colonialism and on historical theory, particularly on the relationship between materialism and the interpretation of historical evidence. His teaching interests also include the history of the African Diaspora. He has taught at Colorado College, Dartmouth College and Stanford University." ;
    ns1:researchOverview "Dr. Hanretta's current research projects are on changing attitudes towards death and marriage among West African Muslims and on the social role of African history teaching in West Africa." .

