        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n540376978>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n583155683>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n772105813>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n879404633>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n883181974> ;
    ns1:overview "Under Dr. Riffee the distance education programs available through the College of Pharmacy have grown.  The College has developed the technology and teamed it with a competency based curriculum and facilitator model which meets the needs of working non-traditional students.  The College now has a Doctor of Pharmacy program for pharmacists with B.S. degrees , Masters in Forensic Science programs, and six Masters programs in Pharmaceutical Outcomes and Policy available as distance learning programs.  The College has also expanded its traditional PharmD  program to three other sites, Jacksonville, Orland, and St. Petersburg doubling the enrollment to 1200 in 2006 from 600 in 2002. Dr. Riffee has been very involved in the College&rsquo;s development of new technological approaches to teaching. Over the last  few years Dr. Riffee has been working with individuals and institutions abroad on the global aspects of education." ;
    ns1:researchOverview "Since 1970, I have been involved in research dealing with the effects of central nervous system stimulants from both the behavioral and biochemical aspects.  The primary focus of my research has been investigating the central effects of amphetamine and apomorphine, both active in the dopaminergic systems of the brain but differing from the aspect of mechanism of action.  A sub-focus of this research has been to study the effects of subchronic administration of these compounds (i.e. given for one or two weeks rather than for months which would be truly chronic administration)." .

