        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n1858772866>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n211059664>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n257612689>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n464104895>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n655400111>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n817418109>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n88511820>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n997415391>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n998749860> ;
    ns1:outreachOverview """<p>I was a member of the Advisory Board for the Agricultural Biotechnology Academy Program at Robert Hungerford Preparatory School in Orlando, FL.&nbsp; This is a public magnet school for gifted students in Orange County. I fund high school apprenticeships most years for students to become exposed to biotechnology research and accomplish science projects.&nbsp; In the last seven years, I have had over 10 apprentices.&nbsp;</p>\r
<p>Apprentices accomplish science projects on their own time and routinely win awards at regional and state science fairs. I established a Biotechnology Internship Program, which was funded by the Florida Grape Growers Association in October 2000. I have used it as the impetus to support one-to-three interns each summer since, with three currently on staff in 2007.</p>""" ;
    ns1:overview "Developmental Biologist specializing in the study of regeneration phenomena and somatic cell genetics of crop plants.&nbsp; The crop of primary interest is grape, with secondary interest devoted to watermelon, melon and grasses.&nbsp; I am pursuing the following research objectives: 1) development of technology to accelerate creation of improved cultivars by incorporating non-conventional approaches such as cell culture, embryo rescue and genetic transformation into contemporary genetic improvement programs, 2) understanding the function of genetic elements in plants and microbes to derive useful genes and controlling elements to be used in crop improvement, 3) determining environmental risks associated with GMO grapevine and developing management strategies as needed." ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "djg@ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(407) 884-2034 ext. 126" ;
    ns1:researchOverview """<p>Developmental Biologist specializing in the study of regeneration phenomena from cells of crop plants. The crop of primary interest is grape, with secondary interest devoted to seedless watermelon and melon. I am pursuing the following research objectives:</p>\r
<p>1) Development of technology to accelerate creation of improved cultivars by incorporating non-conventional approaches such as cell culture, embryo rescue and genetic transformation into contemporary germplasm conservation and improvement programs.</p>\r
<p>2) Understanding the underlying mechanisms that lead to induction of embryogenic plant cells by utilizing highly refined regeneration systems for structural and molecular analyses.</p>""" .

