        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n33008359>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n38707606>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n465591260>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n559701726>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n594747458>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n678532180>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n705730592>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n876367621> ;
    ns1:overview "Dr. Rahim-Williams is a Social and Behavioral Research Scientist. She  holds a Ph.D. in Applied Medical Anthropology, a Master&rsquo;s degree in  Community and Family Health (MPH), and a Master&rsquo;s (MA) degree in Health  Communication from the University of South Florida. She received  training as a Postdoctoral Fellow in pain research from University of  Florida&rsquo;s (UF) Comprehensive Center for Pain Research. Dr.  Rahim-Williams also trained as a UF Claude D. Pepper Scholar in Aging  Research. She is a Fellow of the NIH Summer Institute on Aging Research,  Fellow of the Summer Institute on Community- based participatory  Research and a Fellow of the RAND Summer Institute on Aging Research.  She is the Principal Investigator of a pilot study using games for  health to study pain, physical activity, and functional mobility among  African American and non-Hispanic White women with knee osteoarthritis.  The supplement study was funded by the National Institute of Aging. In  2010, Dr. Rahim-Williams received a five-year career training and  development grant award (K22) to study at the National Institutes of  Health as Senior Research Fellow with the NIH National Institute on  Minority Health and Health Disparities." ;
    ns1:researchOverview "Dr. Rahim-Williams&rsquo; primary research interests are in the areas of  minority women&rsquo;s health and health disparities associated with aging,  pain, and chronic disease self-management. Her primary work addresses  clinical and community-based participatory approaches to investigate  such issues as HIV/AIDS, Type 2 diabetes and osteoarthritis. She is an  ethnographer and uses mixed methods (qualitative/quantitative) in her  research." .

