        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n97170101> ;
    ns1:overview "My research is largely focused on the generation, characterization, and use of mouse models for exploring the pathophysiological mechanisms underlying neurodegenerative diseases and for testing the efficacy of therapies for such diseases.&nbsp; As such, my lab has generated models that are directly relevant for Alzheimer&rsquo;s Disease (AD), Frontotemporal Dementia (FTD), Parkinson&rsquo;s Disease (PD) and Amyotrophic Lateral Sclerosis (ALS).&nbsp; In addition to the amyloid plaques, neurofibrillary tangles (or tangles) composed of phosphorylated, aggregated tau protein are key neuropathological hallmarks of AD.&nbsp; These tau lesions were initially dismissed as being a secondary consequence to the neurodegenerative process in AD until mutations in the MAPT gene encoding the tau protein were associated with Frontotemporal Dementia and parkinsonism linked to chromosome 17 (FTDP-17).&nbsp; FTDP-17 and other neurodegenerative diseases neuropathologically characterized by the accumulation of tangles are classified as tauopathies. This genetic discovery provided the first link that tau dysfunction could be directly causative for neurodegeneration.&nbsp; In collaboration with several laboratories at Mayo Clinic, I generated the first mouse model for neurofibrillary tangle pathology and neurodegeneration that were reminiscent of human FTDP-17.&nbsp; I then utilized this model to demonstrate that the amyloid component of amyloid plaques could directly modify these tangles in vivo, a key finding for the understanding of AD.&nbsp; In collaboration with Dr. Karen Ashe, I co-developed the first conditional mouse model for neurofibrillary tangles which we used to demonstrate that neurofibrillary tangles are not the primary neurotoxic species in tauopathies.&nbsp; My lab is continuing its attempts to identify the main neurotoxic species of tau and we also collaborate with drug companies who are interested in testing their therapeutic agents against tauopathies.&nbsp; We have distributed the transgenic mice and their tissues to ~300 researchers throughout the world.&nbsp; In addition to my extensive program on tauopathies, my lab is currently using a granulin knockout mouse model to study the role of progranulin deficiency in another form of FTD.&nbsp; We have also generated mouse models to study the role of the LRRK2 gene in PD.&nbsp; Finally, we have generated a number of mouse models to understand the role of a key neuropathological hallmark (TDP-43 aggregates) in ALS and FTD." ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "jada.lewis@ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 273-9666" .

