        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n387280699>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n428240481>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n651962929>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n892188663> ;
    ns1:overview """<p>College of Medicine&rsquo;s Department of Psychiatry. Dr. Dizney graduated  from  Southwest Missouri State University before receiving her Doctorate  in Clinical Psychology  from the Forest Institute of Professional  Psychology in Springfield, Missouri.  She completed a pre-doctoral  internship with the Houston Independent School  District and a  Post-Doctoral Residency with the Forensic Unit at the Mental  Health and  Mental Retardation Authority of Harris County.</p>\r
<p>Prior to joining UF,  Dr. Dizney was a professor teaching  undergraduates and mentoring young people  to look toward graduation and  careers in science. She as served as a professor for  graduate level  students, while also engaging in clinical care and service and  has a  history of active participation in academic health care.</p>\r
<p>Dr. Dizney is a  local expert in the treatment of eating  disorders including bulimia nervosa,  binge eating disorder, obesity,  and food addiction. During her career as a licensed  clinical  psychologist, she has specialized in treating individuals in both  residential  and outpatient settings. She has significant experience  providing psychological  care to both adolescents and adults, and  recognizes the importance of family  involvement in clinical care.</p>\r
Dr. Dizney&rsquo;s primary Clinical approach includes  the  development of a new model of mental health treatment to help  individuals overcome  the cognitive and emotional barriers that keep  them from healthy eating. As a  result, the University of Florida Eating  Disorders Program integrates several  theoretical perspectives  including medical monitoring, cognitive-behavioral  therapy,  socio-cultural based interventions, group therapy, along with  participation  in a 12-step based recovery program. She has served as  both a clinician and an  administrator within the State of Florida  mental healthcare system, and has  facilitated changes in statewide  policies to improve the quality of services provided  to youth in the  State of Florida.""" ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "dizney@ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 265-0111 ext. 58233" .

