        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n971833262> ;
    ns1:outreachOverview """<p>Educating the next generation of astronomers is a high priority for Charles Telesco. In the decade since coming to UF, he has been the principal PhD-dissertation adviser for four astronomy students (Elisha Polomski, Scott Fisher, Naibi Marinas, and Margaret Moerchen) and a dissertation-committee member for numerous others in astronomy, physics, and chemistry at UF. He has guided and mentored them toward solid research programs and careers, and he has provided them with a unique experience and opportunity to participate in development of front-line astronomical instrumentation and its deployment and use at the world&rsquo;s largest telescopes.</p>\r
<p>Telesco also developed and taught Observational Techniques as a core graduate course for over five years. More recently, his interest and excitement in teaching nonscience-major undergraduates has grown rapidly. After teaching smaller (30-40 students) AST2007 and AST2008 classes, he has focused on the large (&gt;100 students) AST 1002 classes of introductory astronomy, which he and, by all indications, the students enjoy very much. In this regard, Telesco is beginning to explore more innovative ways&mdash;particularly classroom demonstrations&mdash;to excite these young people about science. As part of his effort to bring science to the broader community, Telesco has given numerous public lectures about astronomy. Among these he has spoken about planet formation and giant-asteroid impacts on the Earth to lay audiences at the University of Cincinnati Sigma Xi public series, at the Gainesville Kiwanas Club, the Miami Gator Club, the UF Sigma Xi series, and as part of the distinguished speakers series at Penn State-Erie in Erie, PA.</p>""" ;
    ns1:overview "I am a Professor in the Department of Astronomy at the University of Florida. I received my PhD in Astronomy and Astrophysics in 1977 from the University of Chicago flying on the Kuiper Airborne Observatory under the guidance of my advisor Al Harper. After a short time at MIT, I went to the University of Hawaii in Manoa as a staff astronomer on the NASA Infrared Telescope Facility, which took me frequently and happily to Mauna Kea. Thereafter, I moved to NASA Ames Research Center, then to NASA Marshall Space Flight Center. In 1995, after 12 years at NASA, I joined the faculty at the University of Florida, where I established the Astronomical Instrumentation Program.<br />" ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "telesco@ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 294-1874" ;
    ns1:researchOverview "My research emphasizes the determination of the detailed structure of circumstellar disks around fairly young stars, those less than a few tens of millions of years old. Disks around stars younger than a million years old are likely still coalescing into planets, and determination of the disk structure provides constraints on the planet-forming environment, particularly the densities and temperatures of the coalescing solids and the shape of the disk. The tools that I use for this research are mid-infrared cameras many of which my team and I at the University of Florida have built. These include OSCIR, T-ReCS, and most recently CanariCam for the 10.4-meter Gran Telescopio Canarias on La Palma in the Canary Islands, Spain. These instruments help fulfill my goal of building the tools that make my ideas come alive." .

