    ns1:overview "Dr. Sue Boinski is an interdisciplinary scientist who uses primates as an entr&eacute;e to the integrative study of evolution, ecology, and social behavior. The research program Boinski first envisioned in 1980, understanding the causes and consequences of geographic variation in the behavioral and community ecology and evolution of Neotropical primates, has become a reality. Field studies of wild capuchins and squirrel monkeys from little-disturbed Neotropical preserves are the nominal focus of this study system, complemented with occasional laboratory-based research. In particular, the team strives to integrate across different scales of biological and cultural processes, and to identify the selective regimes underlying otherwise perplexing patterns in the natural history of behavior, morphology and physiology, and environment. Boinski, along with assistants and collaborators, collects extremely fine-grained behavioral data on individually recognized animals in study troops linked to concurrent ecological sampling of ambitious scope. <br />" ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "boinski@ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 392-2253 ext. 201" ;
    ns1:researchOverview "She studies the ecology and behavior of neotropical primates, and directs the  						Behavioral Ecology of Monkeys at Raleighvallen project in Suriname.  						This locality boasts eight species of sympatric primates. Her research addresses  						questions of the evolution of cognition in primates." .

