        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n967180998> ;
    ns1:overview "I&rsquo;m an associate professor of  anthropology and African studies at the University of Florida where I  teach undergraduate and graduate courses in archaeology and cultural  heritage management. I received my Ph.D in anthropology at the  University of California, Berkeley under the guidance of J. Desmond  Clark, Glynn Ll. Isaac and F. Clark Howell. My doctoral dissertation  focused on documenting and explaining the shift from hunting and  gathering to nomadic pastoralism during the early to mid Holocene in the  southern Afar Rift of Ethiopia. When I am not teaching or doing  research, you can find me at home with my family, fishing in the  backyard lake, playing bocce ball, or traveling the world." ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "sbrandt@ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 392-2253 ext. 234" ;
    ns1:researchOverview "My current research interests lie in  Eastern Africa, where my students, colleagues and I are engaged in  projects on Late Pleistocene refugia and the evolution of modern  cultural behavior, the establishment of agriculture and nomadic  pastoralism, the ethnoarchaeology of lithic production and hide working,  and the protection and conservation of Africa&rsquo;s cultural heritage." .

