        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n549273069>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n65729768>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n828359460>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n85727774> ;
    ns1:overview "D&rsquo;Anieri entered a bachelor&rsquo;s program in international relations at Michigan State, followed by a master&rsquo;s and doctorate in government at Cornell University. His first year in graduate school he took freshman Russian and&mdash;coincidentally&mdash; Daniel O&rsquo;Neill, a UF political scientist, was one of his classmates.<br /><br />In 1991, he was hired as an associate professor of political science at the University of Kansas. After the Soviet Union collapsed, D&rsquo;Anieri won a Fulbright Scholarship to Ukraine, where he picked up a third foreign language while working as a visiting scholar at L&rsquo;viv State University. &ldquo;The university where I worked in Ukraine had no money&mdash;no heat, no chalk, no textbooks&mdash;but they had fantastic students and faculty,&rdquo; he said. &ldquo;That&rsquo;s what matters most.&rdquo; <br /><br />During the summer of 1998, D&rsquo;Anieri was a visiting associate professor at Harvard University&rsquo;s Ukrainian Research Institute. He served as Associate Dean for International Programs at Kansas from 1999 to 2003, Director of the Center for Russian and European Studies in 2003-2004, and Associate Dean of Humanities from 2004 until accepting his new position at UF. <br />" .

