        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n973020116> ;
    ns1:overview "Pamela K. Gilbert received her PhD in English from the University of Southern California in 1994. She has published widely in the areas of Victorian literature, cultural studies and the history of medicine. Her first book, Disease, Desire and the Body in Victorian Women&rsquo;s Popular Novels, was published by Cambridge University Press in 1997, followed by Mapping the Victorian Social Body (SUNY Press, 2004) and The Citizen&rsquo;s Body (Ohio State University Press, 2007), and Cholera and Nation (SUNY Press, 2008). She has edited a collection entitled Imagined Londons (SUNY Press, 2002), and co-edited Beyond Sensation: Mary Elizabeth Braddon in Context (SUNY Press, 1999, with Marlene Tromp and Aeron Haynie).&nbsp; She has also edited a teaching and scholarly edition of Rhoda Broughton&rsquo;s novel, Cometh Up as a Flower (Broadview Press 2010).Professor Gilbert chaired the Department of English from May 2007- May 2011. She teaches courses in the following areas: Victorian literature; feminisms, genders, and sexualities; and cultural studies." ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "pgilbert@ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 294-2828" ;
    ns1:researchOverview "Her research interests include gender, the Victorian novel, the body, Victorian cultural and medical history, and medical humanities." .

