        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n867253217>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n959853339>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n985650614> ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "alys.yng@ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 392-1896" ;
    ns1:researchOverview "I am a biocultural medical anthropologist whose research focuses on the political ecology of maternal and child health and nutrition in African pastoral communities. I am particularly interested in environmental and livestock health in arid and semi-arid environments and understanding how regional climate change, social inequality, and livelihood insecurity shape health equity and patterns of maternal and child health/nutrition in East Africa.<br /><br />My work began with a study of how household economy and social support influence child health and maternal perceptions of infant vulnerability among agropastoral Barabaig (Datoga) living near Lake Eyasi in north-central Tanzania. In particular, I examined how maternal perceptions of infant vulnerability tie to clinical measures of child health and the strategies that women use to balance the needs of the household against the demands of individual children.<br /><br />My current work builds on this research and examines the ways that gender and disparities in allocation of resources within/between Datoga households shapes emotional health, individual perceptions of environmental change and resource security, livelihood strategies, caretaking decisions, and consequent health and nutrition outcomes among women and children in Hanang and Manyara Districts. As part of this work, I am currently involved in a multinational project funded by a seed grant from the Livestock-Climate Change Collaborative Research Support Program (LCC-CRSP) that examines the interactions between climate change, livelihood decisions and child health among pastoral communities in Niger and Tanzania.<br />" .

