        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n898164242>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n914803492> ;
    ns1:overview """<p>I serve as Florida&rsquo;s apiculture extension specialist both for the general public and for beekeepers specifically. I am the outreach coordinator for the Department of Entomology and Nematology. Regarding the former, Florida is home to over 1250 beekeepers who manage ~220,000 bee colonies. Beekeeping is the backbone of Florida and global agriculture due to crop reliance on the pollination services provided by honey bees.</p>\r
<p>Furthermore, Florida is home to Africanized (&ldquo;killer&rdquo;) honey bees that interact regularly with members of the general public. I maintain 3 primary extension programs to address these issues: (1) Partnering with Beekeepers to Improve the Sustainability of Beekeeping, (2) The University of Florida AFBEE Program (African Bee Extension and Education Program) and (3) Insects and Youth: the Department of Entomology and Nematology Outreach Program. My research efforts focus primarily on honey bee pathology, ecology, behavior, and management/husbandry. To that end, I conduct both basic and applied research in these disciplines with an ever-present goal of linking my research to real world situations through my extension programs.</p>\r
<p>With regard to instruction,I oversee graduate student education, serving either as primary supervisor or additional committee member for masters and doctoral students. The research interests of my graduate students vary but focus primarily on apiculture and associated topics. Finally, I regularly supervise undergraduates who pursue independent research credits in my laboratory. Collectively, my duties represent an integrated attempt at addressing apiculture-related problems via research and instruction efforts that are highlighted through targeted extension programs.</p>""" ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "jdellis@ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 273-3924" .

