    ns1:overview "Dr. Lutz teaches qualitative research methods for PhD students and public health nursing for undergraduate students. She also is a Fellow of the American Heart Association and a  certified rehabilitation nurse. She serves on the editorial board of  Rehabilitation Nursing and is a reviewer for several international  journals. Dr. Lutz is chair of the Rehabilitation Nursing Foundation of  the Association of Rehabilitation Nurses (10/10-10/12), and chair of the  Stroke Nursing Committee of the Council on Cardiovascular Nursing for  the American Heart Association (07/10-07/12). She is a member of the  Association of Rehabilitation Nurses, American Heart Association,  American Public Health Association, Florida Nurses Association, and  Sigma Theta Tau International. Dr. Lutz is also a member of the  University of Florida Qualitative Research Community,  http://education.ufl.edu/EdPsych/iiqi/. Clinical practice area/expertise: Dr. Lutz has clinical expertise in  rehabilitation and public health nursing." ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "bjlutz@ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 273-6350" ;
    ns1:researchOverview "Dr. Lutz&rsquo;s research interests are in the areas of access  to community-based health care and support services for vulnerable  populations, in particular services for family caregivers, persons  recovering from stroke, and adults with other chronic, physically  disabling conditions. She has expertise in qualitative research  methodologies, in particular grounded theory. Dr. Lutz completed a  Post-Doctoral Research Fellowship at the Center for Patient-Centered  Interventions at the University of Wisconsin-Madison. She has had a  consistent record of funding including grants from the National  Institutes of Health, the Veterans Administration, and the  Rehabilitation Nursing Foundation. Her current study, which is funded by  the National Institute of Nursing Research, focuses on the needs of  stroke survivors and their family caregivers as they transition home  after inpatient rehabilitation." .

