    ns1:overview "I study evolutionary genetics because I am fascinated by the world around us. With every small breakthrough, more questions emerge. That infinite variety and complexity, accompanied by the illusion that &ldquo;the answer&rdquo; is just around the corner, keeps me in science. At the same time, the interplay between genetic knowledge and society, particularly with respect to ethics, informs my research and teaching." ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "mlwayne@ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 392-9925" ;
    ns1:researchOverview "I study the maintenance of genetic variation, because without it, populations can not evolve. There are two ubiquitous and contradictory observations about traits in natural populations: there is genetic variation for almost every trait ever tested, and most traits are under natural selection. I use the model organism Drosophila melanogaste." .

