        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n353068440>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n383782775>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n38528593>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n408783533>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n424317242>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n433786444>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n499886082>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n516994530>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n524046698>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n552210869>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n553976863>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n620772245>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n651161046>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n683004833>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n745746814>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n748654368>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n749554669>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n774523102>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n782429934>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n844452425>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n86638432>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n871010569>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n886565341>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n899790375>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n903605117>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n927280004>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n962125717>,
        <http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n967796775> ;
    ns1:overview """<p>I&rsquo;m very interested in the use of high throughput screening for the identification of small molecules that interact with proteins to modulate its activity. These small molecules can be natural ligands for the proteins under study or can have a chemical scaffold that is very similar the native regulatory molecule. Along this line I&rsquo;m currently funded through NIH-NIAID (1R03AI078001-01A1) to identify FDA approved molecules that modulate the activity of transcription factors. Using this approach it was possible to identify novobiocin as the specific ligand for a MarR transcription factor (Pagliai et al.,2010) as well as ascorbate as inhibitor of the main acid phosphatase (AcpA) of Francisella tularensis (McRae et al., 2010). This project concerns the study and characterization of protein/proteininteractions. As a model we chose the SspA protein family members (MglA and SspA) which, throughinteraction with RNA polymerase, control the expression of genes required for intramacrophagegrowth, survival and escape. Our hypothesis is that using small molecules to interrupt the interactionbetween transcription regulators the expression of virulence determinants will be inhibited. Thepreliminary data obtained in my laboratory identified quinacrine as the molecule that interfere withSspA/MglA interaction. As a consequence the completion of the project proposed will establish thespecificity and effectiveness of the identified small molecules which will have a broad impact on other select bacterial agents that regulate expression of virulence genes through protein/protein interactions.</p>\r
<p>I have a broad background which I gained during my postdoctoral training completed at the University of California San Diego (mentor: Dr. Milton Saier) and at the University of Toronto (mentor Dr. Aled Edwards). The knowledge obtained through intensive hands on experiments included microbial physiology and metabolism, genetic regulation, microarray analysis, protein purification, enzyme characterization, high-throughput screens of small molecule-protein interaction and chemical biology of transcriptional regulators. The skills acquired are essential for the accomplishment of the research described in this proposal.</p>""" ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "glorca@ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 273-8090" .

