    ns1:overview "Dr. David Mazyck is a Professor in Environmental Engineering Sciences at the University of Florida (UF).&nbsp;" ;
    ns1:primaryEmail "dmazyck@ufl.edu" ;
    ns1:primaryPhoneNumber "(352) 392-6444" ;
    ns1:researchOverview "The overarching theme of my research program is to tailor sorbents for the purification of water and air. In other words, I rely on the fundamentals of adsorption phenomena, chemistry, photocatalysis, and engineering to custom build particles that are more efficient at their task compared to currently available technologies. Current research efforts are primarily focused on photochemistry and photocatalytic particles. The primary objective is to tailor these materials to solve particular problems (e.g., purification of aircraft cabin air, VOCs from paper mills and potential drinking water, and taste and odor causing compounds from drinking water).&nbsp;&nbsp;This approach is different in that other researchers focus on one aspect of a pollution problem or another. For example, carbon scientists study carbon&rsquo;s surface to better understand how pores are produced and why carbons differ with respect to their surface chemistry. Similarly, water chemists focus on solubility of compounds, speciation, etc. Very few researchers combine these fields with the perspective that a better sorbent/particle can be engineered with a thorough understanding of the sorbent and the matrix surrounding it." .

<http://vivo.ufl.edu/individual/n449313857> a ns2:Course,
        ns8:Thing .

